(9) Evading any provision of this or any other valid ordinance of this city relating to the retail sale of distilled spirits by the drink for consumption on the premises where sold, served, or furnished for consumption on such premises, by whatever false schedule, trick or device may be employed;
(10) Making gratuitous gifts or donations of spurious memberships in an alleged private, regular, dues-paying members club, or including enough not specified in the price charged for purchases of merchandise or privileges, other than distilled spirits, to cover membership dues, shall be held an evasion, and shall not be treated as a bona fide donation or gift;
(11) Aiding, abetting, counseling, advising, or assisting in the violation of this or any other applicable valid city ordinance relating to the sale at retail of any distilled spirits by the drink for consumption on the premises where sold.
(Ord. No. 031-13, 12-16-13)

1499:
(a) It shall be unlawful for private clubs licensed hereunder to fail to meet the following requirements:
(1) No member shall be limited or restricted in his or her authority, control, or power within three (3) months from the date he or she becomes such a member;
(2) All private clubs shall make and keep a correct and true printed or typewritten list of all of its members open to inspection of the city license inspector, mayor, all members of the city council, the city manager, and any member of the police department of the city, at all times of day and night when the club is open to its members);
(3) All private clubs shall confine membership to persons over twenty-one (21) years of age;
(4) All private clubs shall restrict their patronage to their bona fide regular dues-paying members;
(5) All private clubs shall derive a majority of their income from their bona fide regular membership dues, sales and service of meals, and private dances by such members and their immediate families, or other lawful activities not related to or involving the sale or use of alcoholic beverages;
(6) Private clubs shall not derive a majority of their income from the sale of alcoholic beverages;
(7) No private club shall be organized or operated primarily for the selling or serving of alcoholic beverages by the drink;
(8) All private clubs shall have at least one hundred (100) members regularly paying dues, organized and operated exclusively for pleasure, recreation and other non-profitable purposes, no part of the net earnings of which inure to the benefit of any shareholder or member and owning, hiring or leasing a building or space therein for the reasonable use of its members with suitable kitchen and dining room space and equipment and maintaining and using a sufficient number of employees for cooking, preparing and serving meals for its members and guests; provided that no member or officer, agent or employee, is paid or directly or indirectly receives in any form of salary or other compensation any profits from the sale of distilled spirits, wines, champagnes, or malt beverages beyond the amount of such salary as may be fixed by its members at an annual meeting, or by its governing body, out of the general revenue of the club. For the purposes of this subsection, tips which are added to the bill under club regulations shall not be considered as profits hereunder.
(b) The prohibitions contained in section 11-1430 shall specifically apply herein.
(Ord. No. 031-13, 12-16-13)

1503:
(a) Organizers of special events to be conducted within the city must first file an application with the police chief, or designee, for a permit authorizing the event. If alcoholic beverages are to be served, the application shall so state and shall designate a licensee of the city authorized to sell alcoholic beverages by the drink as the events alcohol vendor. An application fee shall be paid with the application, and a permit fee shall be paid before issuance of the permit. The application fee and permit fee shall be established by written policy of the police chief after consultation with the mayor and city council. After investigation by the police chief or his/her designee, the police chief, or his/her designee, may issue a special event permit to an individual or organization for a special event which may include a permit for the sale of alcoholic beverages for consumption within the controlled zone only during the approved special event under the following conditions:
(1) The applicant must contract with an alcohol licensee who holds an annual license for the sale of alcoholic beverages for on-premises consumption within the city, who will hold the special alcohol permit and be responsible for the sale of alcoholic beverages at the special event.;
(2) The special event alcohol permit may be issued to the licensee and will allow sale of alcoholic beverages beyond the premises described in the licensee's annual license only in the controlled zone specifically described in the application as approved in the permit, and only during the special event approved;
(3) Food must be served within the controlled zone during any period of time that alcoholic beverages are served;
(4) The application for such special event and special alcohol permit must have been filed with the police chief or his/her designee, at least thirty (30) days prior to the date of the special event;
(5) All applicants and permit holders must comply with all state statutes governing the sale of alcoholic beverages and all sections of this code and other city ordinances governing such sales, including but not limited to sections of this code and other ordinances relating to the hours of operation, distance requirements and the permit requirements for employees contained in the City of East Point Ordinances;
(6) The application shall state the dates and times for the special event;
(7) To qualify as a special event, the proposed event must be open to the general public, subject to reasonable attendance restrictions. As part to the application process, the applicant must provide a copy of the event's attendance policy and a copy of the event's marketing plan including copies of marketing materials. The application shall include an estimate of public attendance for each one-half (½) day of the event. The application shall also include a security plan for the event in form required by the chief of police. The security plan must provide for contingencies, such as attendance in excess of projected attendance, and unexpected public safety issues during the event. The police chief may place conditions and requirements upon the event as part of an approved security plan. The police chief may restrict the number of event attendees allowed within the controlled zone, and may limit attendance during the special event based upon conditions within the controlled zone and the effectiveness of the security plan. The police chief may require that the applicant hire an off-duty city police officer to be onsite within the controlled zone during the special event.
(b) Special event organizers may contract with any on-premises license holder of the city to sell alcoholic beverages at the special event, who shall hold the special alcohol permit.
(c) A special event permit and special alcohol permit may be immediately revoked by the police chief (or his/her designee) for a violation of state or city laws, inadequate security, or unruly attendees which results in an emergency situation in which continued operation of the premises by the licensee may, in the opinion of the police chief, (or his/her designee), endanger the health, welfare or safety of the public, the attendees, or city or private property.
(Ord. No. 031-13, 12-16-13)

1520:
Application for a license for operating an amusement game room within the corporate limits of the City of East Point shall be made to the city manager upon a form to be supplied by the city manager for this purpose. The license application shall include the following information;
(a) Name, address, and age of the applicant, and date of the application;
(b) Address or place where the bona fide coin operated amusement machine or machines are to be offered to the public for play and the other business or businesses operated at that address or place;
(c) Name and address of the owner of the machine or machines and a copy of the owner's master license;
(d) Name and address of any other business owned or operated by applicant within the corporate limits of the city; and
(e) List of any other licenses or permits from the city held by the applicant.
Upon issuing a license for an amusement game room, the city official or employee shall provide the licensee with a copy of this ordinance. The city shall require a fee for licensure or registration of an amusement game room in accordance with its business license requirements. A license issued in accordance with this chapter shall be valid until December 31 of the year in which the license was issued. The owner or operator of an amusement game room shall be required to pay occupation taxes in accordance with section 5-2001 et. seq. of the Code of Ordinances.
(Ord. No. 016-08, Art. 1, 4-7-08)
